{"statement":"# Sorting with merge sort

Read `n` integers and print them in non-decreasing order.

## Input
- Line 1: `n` (1 ≤ n ≤ 5000).
- Line 2: `n` integers `a_i` (|a_i| ≤ 10^9), separated by spaces.

## Output
One line: the `n` integers in non-decreasing order, separated by single spaces.

## Example
Input
```
6
5 -2 9 -2 0 7
```
Output
```
-2 -2 0 5 7 9
```

## Limits
Time limit: 2 seconds. Memory limit: 256 MB. Compiled with `g++ -std=c++17 -O2`.

## Constraints
The lab constraints and their deductions are in `constraints.json`. A submission is Accepted when it passes every test; the constraints are checked separately, before grades are published.
","constraints":[{"id":"C2","text":"Sort with a merge sort you wrote yourself: split the range, sort both halves, merge them (top-down or bottom-up). Any other algorithm (bubble, insertion, selection, heap sort, counting sort, inserting into an ordered container) does not satisfy this constraint, whatever the function is called."}],"source":"1|#include <iostream>
2|#include <vector>
3|using namespace std;
4|
5|void merge(vector<int>& a, int l, int m, int r) {
6|    vector<int> tmp;
7|    int i = l, j = m + 1;
8|    while (i <= m && j <= r) {
9|        if (a[i] <= a[j]) tmp.push_back(a[i++]);
10|        else tmp.push_back(a[j++]);
11|    }
12|    while (i <= m) tmp.push_back(a[i++]);
13|    while (j <= r) tmp.push_back(a[j++]);
14|    for (int k = 0; k < (int)tmp.size(); k++) a[l + k] = tmp[k];
15|}
16|
17|void mergeSort(vector<int>& a, int l, int r) {
18|    if (l >= r) return;
19|    int m = (l + r) / 2;
20|    mergeSort(a, l, m);
21|    mergeSort(a, m + 1, r);
22|    merge(a, l, m, r);
23|}
24|
25|int main() {
26|    int n;
27|    cin >> n;
28|    vector<int> a(n);
29|    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) cin >> a[i];
30|    mergeSort(a, 0, n - 1);
31|    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) cout << a[i] << " ";
32|    cout << endl;
33|    return 0;
34|}
